re the system is prepared using ansible modules. This includes gathering facts and installing packages using the package module. It uses become and become_user variables. I\'ll loop over bootstrap__facts_packages to install each package. The become parameters are optional, so I\'ll include them with defaults if necessary.\n\nNow, putting it all together, I\'ll structure each task as a separate entry in the playbook. I\'ll make sure each task uses the correct module, parameters, and conditions. I\'ll also ensure that the YAML syntax is correct, with proper indentation and no trailing spaces.\n\nI should double-check each task against the user\'s instructions to ensure I haven\'t missed any details. For example, in Task 3, the block includes a command to test connectivity, registers the result, and then conditionally includes other tasks. Also, in Task 4, the loop is correctly applied to the packages.\n\nFinally, I\'ll write out the YAML, starting with \'---\', followed by each task as described, making sure there are no extra comments or explanations, just the valid YAML structure as per the user\'s request.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Check all variables have been set correctly\n  include_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n  vars:\n    remote_host: localhost\n  changed_when: false\n\n- name: Wait for host (when configured)\n  wait_for:\n    port: "{{ ansible_port }}"\n    host: "{{ ansible_ssh_host | default(inventory_hostname) | default(\'localhost\') }}"\n    timeout: "{{ bootstrap_timeout }}"\n    state: present\n  when: ansible_connection not in [\'container\', \'docker\', \'community.docker.docker\']\n\n- name: Prepare the system\n  block:\n    - name: Check connection\n      command: sh -c \'echo > /dev/null\'\n      ignore_errors: yes\n      register: connection_test_result\n      changed_when: false\n\n    - name: Log connection test result\n      debug:\n        msg: "Connection test result: {{ connection_test_result | json_summary }}"\n      when: connec