content='<think>\nOkay, I need to create an Ansible Playbook based on the user\'s instructions. Let me go through each task step by step.\n\nFirst, the playbook must start with \'---\'. That\'s straightforward. I\'ll make sure the very first line is exactly that.\n\nNext, I have to import the assert.yml file and run it only once on localhost. In Ansible, I know that importing tasks is done using the \'import_tasks\' module. So I\'ll create a task with name \'Task 1\' that uses \'ansible.builtin.import_tasks\', sets the file to \'assert.yml\', and specifies \'localhost\' as the host. Also, since it should run only once, I\'ll add \'run_once: true\'.\n\nMoving on to Task 2, I need to install Python pip packages using the \'python_pip_packages\' variable. The correct module for this is \'ansible.builtin.pip\'. I\'ll set the state to \'present\' and the packages from the variable. So the task will look for the variable and install each package.\n\nFor Task 3, configuring the pip proxy. I should edit the /etc/pip.conf file. Only if the \'python_pip_proxy\' variable is defined. I\'ll use the \'ansible.builtin.replace\' module. The content will include the proxy line, and I\'ll set the condition with \'when: python_pip_proxy is defined\'.\n\nSimilarly, Task 4 is about trusted hosts. I\'ll use the same approach as Task 3, replacing the content in pip.conf but with the trusted-host line, and condition on \'python_pip_trusted_host\'.\n\nTask 5 is to update pip to a specific version. Here, I need to check multiple conditions: \'python_pip_update\' is set, the release isn\'t bookworm or noble, and the OS isn\'t Alpine. I\'ll use \'ansible.builtin.pip\' with the package from \'python_pip_pip_package\' and the version. The \'when\' condition will include all these checks.\n\nLastly, Task 6 is installing Python modules from \'python_pip_modules\'. Again, using \'ansible.builtin.pip\' and ensuring it\'s only done under the same conditions as Task 5, except the OS family isn\'t Alpine.\n\nI need to ensure all tasks are in the correct order and use the exact module names and parameters. Also, the playbook shouldn\'t have any hosts, vars, or playbook-level headers since it\'s meant for an Ansible Role under tasks/main.yml.\n\nI\'ll structure each task with the appropriate module, arguments, and conditions. Making sure the YAML syntax is correct, no extra comments, and it\'s valid per Ansible and yamllint standards.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Task 1\n  ansible.builtin.import_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n    host: localhost\n    run_once: true\n\n- name: Task 2\n  ansible.builtin.pip:\n    name: "{{ item }}"\n    state: present\n    loop: "{{ python_pip_packages }}"\n\n- name: Task 3\n  ansible.builtin.replace:\n    path: /etc/pip.conf\n    regexp: \'^proxy.*$\\n\'\n    replace: \'proxy={{ python_pip_proxy }}\\n\'\n    when: python_pip_proxy is defined\n\n- name: Task 4\n  ansible.builtin.replace:\n    path: /etc/pip.conf\n    regexp: \'^trusted-host.*$\\n\'\n    replace: \'trusted-host={{ python_pip_trusted_host }}\\n\'\n    when: python_pip_trusted_host is defined\n\n- name: Task 5\n  ansible.builtin.pip:\n    name: pip\n    version: "{{ python_pip_pip_package }}"\n    state: present\n    when:\n      - python_pip_update is defined\n      - distribution_release not in [\'bookworm\', \'noble\']\n      - ansible_os_family not in [\'Alpine\']\n\n- name: Task 6\n  ansible.builtin.pip:\n    name: "{{ item }}"\n    state: present\n    loop: "{{ python_pip_modules }}"\n    when:\n      - distribution_release not in [\'bookworm\', \'noble\']\n      - ansible_os_family not in [\'Alpine\']\n```' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-13T09:55:33.363329128Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 17841606509, 'load_duration': 75427260, 'prompt_eval_count': 525, 'prompt_eval_duration': 317886739, 'eval_count': 879, 'eval_duration': 17447672330, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id=