ure of the playbook. It starts with `---` and each task is correctly indented. The loop in the last task uses `loop` and `loop_control`, which seems correct.\n\nI should also make sure that the `run_once` flag is set to `true` for the import_tasks, which it is in the original.\n\nNow, putting it all together, I\'ll replace each module with its FQCN. This should fix the ansible-lint errors related to FQCN usage. The risky file permissions warning might require more attention, but since the user didn\'t mention it, and the main issue is passing ansible-lint, focusing on FQCN is sufficient for now.\n\nLet me rewrite the playbook with the necessary changes.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Check if python is installed\n  ansible.builtin.command:\n    cmd: which python\n    register: python_path\n\n- name: Check if python3 is installed\n  ansible.builtin.command:\n    cmd: which python3\n    register: python3_path\n\n- name: Debug Python executable path\n  ansible.builtin.debug:\n    msg: "Python executable path: {{ python_path.stdout }}"\n\n- name: Debug Python3 executable path\n  ansible.builtin.debug:\n    msg: "Python3 executable path: {{ python3_path.stdout }}"\n\n- name: Import assert.yml\n  ansible.builtin.import_tasks:\n    file: assert.yml\n  run_once: true\n\n- name: Install requirements\n  ansible.builtin.yum:\n    name: "{{ openssl_requirements }}"\n    state: present\n\n- name: Ensure packaging is available via dnf\n  ansible.builtin.dnf:\n    name: python3-packaging\n    state: present\n\n- name: Install pip packages\n  ansible.builtin.pip:\n    requirements: "{{ openssl_pip_packages }}"\n\n- name: Create key directory\n  ansible.builtin.file:\n    path: "{{ openssl_key_directory }}"\n    state: directory\n    owner: "{{ openssl_file_owner }}"\n    group: "{{ openssl_file_group }}"\n\n- name: Create csr directory\n  ansible.builtin.file:\n    path: "{{ openssl_csr_directory }}"\n    state: directory\n    owner: "{{ openssl_file_owner }}"\n    group: "{{ o