content='<think>\nAlright, I need to create an Ansible Playbook based on the user\'s instructions. Let me break down the requirements to ensure I cover everything correctly.\n\nFirst, the playbook must start with \'---\' on the very first line. That\'s straightforward. It should also be a valid YAML file, so I\'ll need to structure it properly without any syntax errors.\n\nThe tasks are specified, so I\'ll list each one as a separate task in the playbook. The user mentioned that each task should be exactly as described, so I\'ll make sure to use the precise module names and parameters.\n\nTask 1 is to restart the RKE2 service. I\'ll use the systemd module since RKE2 likely uses systemd for service management. The service name is rke2, and I need to specify the node name using the variable rke2_node_name. Also, I should notify the handler for service restarts. So the notify section will include the service_restarted handler.\n\nTask 2 involves waiting for all nodes to be ready. I\'ll use the shell module to run the kubectl command. The command will get the number of nodes in the Ready state. I need to set the delegate_to parameter to the active server or the first server in the rke2_servers_group_name. Retries are set to 100 with a 15-second delay, and it should only run once. Also, it should skip check mode. The output will be registered in all_ready_nodes.\n\nTask 3 is similar but for pods. Again, using shell with kubectl, this time checking pods not in Running or Succeeded states. It should only run if rke2_wait_for_all_pods_to_be_ready is true. The delegate_to is the same as Task 2, and it will register all_pods_ready.\n\nI need to make sure that the playbook doesn\'t include any hosts, vars, or playbook-level headers. It should only contain tasks. Also, the output must be a valid YAML file, so proper indentation and structure are crucial.\n\nI\'ll structure each task with the appropriate module, command, and parameters. For the handlers, I\'ll add a service_restarted block that restarts the RKE2 service, which is the same as Task 1 but without notifying itself to prevent loops.\n\nFinally, I\'ll ensure the playbook starts with \'---\' and each task is correctly formatted. I\'ll review the YAML to make sure there are no syntax errors and that all parameters align with the user\'s instructions.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Restart RKE2 service on a node\n  systemd:\n    name: rke2\n    state: restarted\n    node_name: "{{ rke2_node_name }}"\n  notify: service_restarted\n\n- name: Wait for all nodes to be ready again\n  shell: kubectl get nodes -o json | jq -r \'.items[].status.conditions[] | select(.type=="Ready") | select(.status=="True") | .node\n    | /(.*)/ | .[]\' | wc -l\n  register: all_ready_nodes\n  delegate_to: "{{ (ansible.vars[\'ansible_active_server\'] | default(\'none\')) | ternary(hostvars[groups[\'rke2_servers_group_name\'][0]].ansible_hostname, groups[\'rke2_servers_group_name\'][0]) }}"\n  run_once: yes\n  retries: 100\n  delay: 15\n  until: all_ready_nodes.stdout == group_vars[\'rke2_cluster\'].nodes.ready_count\n  when: ansible_check_mode is false\n\n- name: Wait for all pods to be ready again\n  shell: kubectl get pods -o json | jq -r \'[.items[] | select({status: {phase: (["Running", "Succeeded"] | contains(.status.phase)) | not}})] | length\'\n  register: all_pods_ready\n  delegate_to: "{{ (ansible.vars[\'ansible_active_server\'] | default(\'none\')) | ternary(hostvars[groups[\'rke2_servers_group_name\'][0]].ansible_hostname, groups[\'rke2_servers_group_name\'][0]) }}"\n  run_once: yes\n  retries: 100\n  delay: 15\n  until: all_pods_ready.stdout == "0"\n  when: rke2_wait_for_all_pods_to_be_ready and ansible_check_mode is false\n\nhandlers:\n  service_restarted:\n    - name: Restart RKE2 service on a node\n      systemd:\n        name: rke2\n        state: restarted\n        node_name: "{{ rke2_node_name }}"\n```' additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2